================================================================================
Third-party components and data
================================================================================

Deej-AI
  https://github.com/teticio/Deej-AI                                  (GPL-3.0)
deej-ai.online-app
  https://github.com/teticio/deej-ai.online-app                       (GPL-3.0)

  The recommendation algorithm in internal/reco/deejai is a Go port of
  backend/deejai.py from deej-ai.online-app.

  The bundled track-embedding catalog is derived from the pre-computed datasets
  published by the Deej-AI project (spotifytovec.p, tracktovec.p,
  spotify_tracks.p, spotify_urls.p). A written offer of the corresponding source
  and data-conversion tooling (python/convert_pickles.py) accompanies every
  distribution that ships the converted catalog.

Track metadata is derived from data originating with Spotify. Only 30-second
preview URLs and derived embedding vectors are redistributed; no audio and no
cover art.

MusicBrainz
  https://musicbrainz.org

  Queried at runtime for ISRC and release metadata under the MusicBrainz API
  terms. Every request identifies this application with a contact URL as those
  terms require.

Deezer
  https://developers.deezer.com

  The public search API is queried at runtime to resolve optional 30-second
  preview URLs. No API key is used.

Soundiiz
  https://soundiiz.com

  Optional playlist export uses Soundiiz's public handoff endpoint
  (POST https://soundiiz.com/go/import-playlist). Only the playlist title,
  description, and track/artist names are sent; the returned share URL is opened
  in the user's browser to complete the transfer. No account or token is used by
  this app, and no streaming credentials pass through it. The mechanism follows
  https://github.com/platten/playlistforge.

================================================================================
Bundled binaries
================================================================================

llama.cpp   https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp                          (MIT)
  The optional local intent parser runs `llama-server`. Every installer and
  portable archive bundles a pinned CPU-inference build of it (see
  build/llama/manifest.json for the exact release tag and per-OS SHA-256;
  build/llama/fetch.sh downloads, verifies, and stages it at package time) next
  to the app binary — the first place ai.llama_server_path resolution looks.
  Its own MIT license text ships alongside it (llama.cpp-LICENSE, e.g. under
  /usr/share/doc/playlist-ai on Linux). Model weights (GGUF) are NOT bundled —
  the user obtains them on first launch, directly from their source, and
  accepts that model's license at that time.
